<DocsShell nav={data?.nav} current="/docs/concepts/" {toc}>
<article id="top" class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs · Concepts</p>
  <h1>One seed, three realms.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Everything in a FrontierJS app derives from one file, <code>db/schema.lite</code>.
    The rest grows outward from it, and every part can be traced back. This page is
    the handful of ideas that make that true. Read it before anything else; the
    guides assume it.
  </p>

  <!-- ─── The schema ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="schema">
    <h2>The schema is the seed</h2>
    <p>
      A schema states what is true about the data for its whole life: the fields and
      their rules, the relations, who may touch it, and how it may change. Here is one
      model. Every section below points back at a line of it.
    </p>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.LEAD}</pre>
    <p>From those lines, without anyone writing them again:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The table</strong> and every migration after it.</li>
      <li><strong>Validation</strong>: a bad email is refused before it reaches the database, on the server and in the browser, with the same rule.</li>
      <li><strong>Access</strong>: who may read, create, update and delete, and which rows each person sees.</li>
      <li><strong>The lifecycle</strong>: the only ways <code>status</code> can change.</li>
      <li><strong>Types, a JSON Schema, a default form and an agent's tools</strong>, each a projection of the same model.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      What the schema never holds is UI behavior or business logic. It says what is
      true, not what to do about it, and that limit is what keeps everything derived
      from it predictable.
    </p>
    <p class="from">See it: <a href="/tour/derived/">Derived</a>, one model and everything the schema produces.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Three realms ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="realms">
    <h2>Three realms, three nouns</h2>
    <p>
      An app has three realms, and each has one noun. Learn the three and you can
      predict how a feature works before reading its docs.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Realm</th><th>Noun</th><th>What it is</th><th>For a lead</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Data</td><td><strong>Model</strong></td><td>What the app knows, and the rules that govern it</td><td><code>model Lead</code>, reached as <code>db.lead</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>API</td><td><strong>Service</strong></td><td>What the outside world may ask for, and how</td><td><code>leads.service.ts</code>, served at <code>/api/leads</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>UI</td><td><strong>Resource</strong></td><td>How a screen binds to a service</td><td><code>Lead.mesa</code>, exporting <code>leads</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The Service and the Resource for <code>Lead</code> are each a few lines, because
      everything they could say is already in the model:
    </p>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.SERVICE}</pre>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.RESOURCE}</pre>
    <p>
      That empty service answers find, get, create, update, patch and remove, refuses
      bad input, grades every caller and filters the list by the row policy. A file in
      a realm holds only what the model cannot say.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two more nouns describe what an app becomes rather than what it is: a
      <strong>Release</strong> is how it ships, and a <strong>Suite</strong> is how it
      is verified.
    </p>
    <p class="from">See it: <a href="/tour/walkthrough/">The walkthrough</a>, one model becoming a database, an API and a UI.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Declare first ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="declare">
    <h2>Declare first, then write code</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>Declaration</strong> is something the app states and the framework
      enforces: a model, a gate, a transition, a service's list of methods. A
      <strong>Hook</strong> is code that runs. Prefer the first, because a declaration
      is read by every part of the app at once, and code is read only where it runs.
    </p>
    <p>When an app needs more, there are four ways in, tried in this order:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Declaration</strong>: change the data's own rules in the schema.</li>
      <li><strong>Hook</strong>: step into one call's pipeline, before or after the work.</li>
      <li><strong>Plugin</strong>: attach a capability to the whole app, such as jobs or integrations.</li>
      <li><strong>Provider</strong>: swap the implementation behind a seam, such as where mail is sent.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Each step is wider than the one before it. Reaching for a hook where a
      declaration would do means the rule lives in one service and nowhere else.
    </p>
    <p class="from">See it: <a href="/pitch/#extend">The pitch, Extend</a>, with a sample of each.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Access ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="access">
    <h2>Access lives on the model</h2>
    <p>
      Who may do what is declared in the schema and enforced where the data is
      written and read. A route, a job, a script and an agent all reach the data
      through the same check, so none of them can forget it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every caller is graded to a level on a ladder from 0 to 9, and
      <code>@@gate</code> names the level each operation needs, in the order read,
      create, update, delete:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ladder">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>STRANGER</td><td>5</td><td>ADMINISTRATOR</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>VISITOR</td><td>6</td><td>OWNER</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>READER</td><td>7</td><td>SYSADMIN</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>CREATOR</td><td>8</td><td>SYSTEM, reached only by <code>asSystem()</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>USER</td><td>9</td><td>LOCKED, which nothing passes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Reading <code>Lead</code> top to bottom:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>@@gate(2.4.4.5)</code>: a READER may read, a USER may create and update, an ADMINISTRATOR may delete. A stranger gets nothing.</li>
      <li><code>@@allow(read, ownerId == auth().id)</code>: a row policy. It is compiled into the query, so a USER's list holds only their own leads.</li>
      <li><code>@encrypted</code> on <code>phone</code>: stored encrypted and left out of every ordinary read.</li>
      <li><code>@@transitions</code>: the only moves <code>status</code> can make. Contacting a lead twice is refused, and one move can carry a gate of its own.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The browser receives the same rules, so a screen can hide a button the user
      cannot press. That is a courtesy and nothing more: the server enforces the rule
      whatever the screen shows. A schema that declares no access rule at all is an
      open, plain ORM; once it declares one, a model with no gate is reachable only by
      the system. <code>asSystem()</code> is the one way past a gate, and it is
      audited.
    </p>
    <p class="from">See it: <a href="/tour/field/#data">One field</a>, from the model through each boundary.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── The chain ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="chain">
    <h2>The chain has a direction</h2>
    <p>
      A call travels down as data and a query, and comes back up as a result or an
      error. Each realm meets the next at a <strong>Boundary</strong>, and each
      boundary is a named checkpoint, not a wall: the caller is graded, input is
      checked, and the result is wrapped in one envelope.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every translation along the way has exactly one owner. One place turns an error
      into an HTTP status. One place reads <code>?$limit=20</code> off a URL and hands
      the service a directive, and nothing past that point sees the
      <code>$</code>. When something comes out wrong, there is one place to look.
    </p>
    <p class="from">See it: <a href="/tour/seams/">The seams</a>, one POST and every handoff it crosses.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Change ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="change">
    <h2>Change flows outward</h2>
    <p>
      Every fact has one origin, the row. When a row is written, the write is
      announced once as an <strong>Event</strong> on a <strong>Channel</strong>, and
      every screen bound to that channel updates. A write
      that went through no service, from a job or a script, is still announced,
      because the announcement comes from the data and not from the caller.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the UI binds; it does not own. A screen holds a reference to the data, never
      a second copy that has to be kept in step, and live updates are how every app
      behaves rather than a feature one adds.
    </p>
    <p class="from">See it: <a href="/tour/ripple/">The ripple</a>, one line changed and what moves.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Surfaces ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="surfaces">
    <h2>Surfaces sit beside the schema</h2>
    <p>
      An app's folder is <code>db/</code> and, beside it, one folder per
      <strong>surface</strong>: the places the app is reached from.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>api/</code></td><td>The services, over HTTP and WebSocket</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>web/</code></td><td>The application people sign in to</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>site/</code></td><td>A public site, built to static pages</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>widgets/</code></td><td>Pieces embedded in somebody else's page</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>extension/</code></td><td>A browser extension</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>desktop/</code> · <code>mobile/</code></td><td>Native apps</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>cli/</code></td><td>A command line and terminal UI</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Every interface is written in Mesa, and a surface is where its compiled output
      goes, so a component reads the same in all of them. <code>shared/</code> is a
      root but not a surface: pure code two surfaces both import, which imports
      neither.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Axioms ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="axioms">
    <h2>Three axioms</h2>
    <p>Everything above is one of three rules, applied again:</p>
    <ul class="axioms">
      <li><strong>One origin.</strong> A fact is stated once, and everything else is derived from it. <a href="#schema">The schema</a>, <a href="#change">change</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>One name.</strong> A thing has one name in every realm, so <code>Lead</code>, <code>db.lead</code>, <code>/api/leads</code> and <code>leads</code> are found from each other. <a href="#realms">Three realms</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>One owner.</strong> Each rule and each translation lives in one place. <a href="#access">Access</a>, <a href="#chain">the chain</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <footer class="next">
    <p><strong>Next:</strong> <a href="/docs/ecosystem/">Ecosystem</a>, the packages around an app. <a href="/tour/">The tour</a>, each idea shown running. <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/VOCABULARY.md">Vocabulary</a>, every defined word.</p>
  </footer>
</article>
</DocsShell>

